Let G be a finite metric graph, i.e. a connected graph whose edges are identified with intervals of R (some possibly half-lines), endowed with the natural arc-length measure. On G consider the focusing nonlinear Schrödinger (NLS) equation
   i ∂_t Ψ = -Δ_V Ψ - |Ψ|^{2σ} Ψ,    σ > 0,
where Δ_V denotes the Laplacian on G defined by a self-adjoint vertex coupling V at each vertex v. The standard Kirchhoff (free) coupling requires continuity of the wavefunction at v together with the vanishing of the sum of the outgoing derivatives. More general self-adjoint couplings include:
 (a) δ-couplings of strength α: continuity of Ψ at v and Σ ∂Ψ_e(v) = α Ψ(v);
 (b) δ′-couplings: continuity of the outgoing derivatives at v and a jump condition Σ Ψ_e(v) = β ∂Ψ(v);
 (c) more general U(d)-parametrized couplings at a vertex of degree d.
A standing wave is a solution of the form Ψ(t,x) = e^{iωt} φ(x) with φ ∈ H^1(G) a real-valued bound state of the stationary equation -Δ_V φ + ω φ = |φ|^{2σ} φ; a ground state is a minimizer of the associated NLS energy under fixed L^2-mass.

Question: For NLS on a metric graph G equipped with non-Kirchhoff vertex conditions (in particular δ- or δ′-type couplings, or other self-adjoint couplings), determine whether such couplings give rise to:
 (i) new branches of standing waves not present in the Kirchhoff case;
 (ii) bifurcations of the ground state as the mass (or frequency) parameter varies;
 (iii) spontaneous symmetry-breaking phenomena, in analogy with the known symmetry-breaking bifurcation of the ground state established for the NLS on the real line with a δ′ point interaction.

Characterize, in terms of the graph topology and the vertex coupling parameters, the existence, multiplicity, and orbital (in)stability of such standing-wave branches and bifurcation points.

